A||N||17||PocketRocket||Handling is kind of wavy...?||||4||
Z||000000||PocketRocket||10-08-2000||05:47 PM||||Is there anything that can be done to stop this. At high sped it tends to wiggle when I hit bumps in the road.  Feels like I am about to lose control and its annoying.  Or is it probably because I am used to driving my Power Steering equipped Mustang?<P>------------------<BR>-Larry B<BR>'85 SE 2.8L||||reg||4
Z||000001||Songman||10-08-2000||07:56 PM||||That's typical with my experience. When you think about how a Fiero is setup it makes sense. You have air coming through the radiator in the front of the car with nowhere to go. This lifts the front of the car since there is no weight there. At very high speeds your headlight doors may even come up some.<P>Some people are now putting vents in the front of the hood to let the air escape. I'll probably do that when someone has a bolt-on hood with scoops for sale. Also, I'm thinking of adding one of the sterring dampening setups too.||||reg||1
Z||000002||My7Fieros||10-08-2000||09:04 PM||||Yup, what Songman said. Also, you may need to replace your control arm bushings...not too big of an ordeal (I hope, because Im getting ready to do mine, or have it done).||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bob Englert||10-08-2000||09:48 PM||||Nice to see another NC Fiero owner.  You missed a great event at the recent Run For the Hills #5 in Murphy, NC last week.  I keep asking if anyone in NC would like to get together somewhere to meet and talk Fieros.  Hard to get much of a response.<P>Does the front or rear feel like it is moving around.  I have owned several Fieros and when the supension was in good shape they are solidly planted.  I just picked up 2 GT's with worm out suspensions that wander all over the road when they hit any bump in the road.<P>My daughter's 87 Coupe felt like it had 4 wheel steer when we bought it.  The front control arm bushins were completely worn out and the left rear toe link (inner tie rod) had about 1/4" of play.  It turns out the rear wheel was steering itself!<P>Suspension rebuilds are fairly easy to do on the Fiero.  I have done 2 pre 88's and 1 88 Formula so far.  Both GT's will need it done.  Parts for the 84-87 cars are readily available.<P>If the suspension is in good shape I would suggest you have the alignment checked carfully.  Be sure to have a 4 wheel alignment.  Fiero's are very sensitive to alignment.  Make sure the shop understands how to do the entire job, not just set the front and rear toe.<P>I don't think I have seen a Fiero with 100K miles that had a good original suspension in it.  They seem to wear hard in this area.<P>Would be happy to answer any specific questions.||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||10-09-2000||07:32 AM||||PocketRocket, there are a couple of things that you could be feeling.  The first is the front end lift that songman mentioned, and Bob talked about the bushings.  This is probably where most of the problem is. <P>When driving... wiggle the wheel, do you get a feeling of the rear still swamping around?  Chances are, this is mostly what you are feeling, this is caused rubber cradle bushings that are worn out.  These need to be replaced.<P>You also need to change the bushings on the front swaybar.  I just did mine with a cheap kit from AutoZone... big difference! <P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||bHooper||10-09-2000||07:34 AM||||Hey, Bob.  How you doing?  You may or may not remeber me, I came by and took that Formula for a test drive last spring.  It got me motivated to get my own car back together. <P>Email me sometime.<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||03-11-2001||02:16 AM||||I was buzzing this thread looking for some solutions. Seems like there is a certain amount of guessing going on.<P>One of my 86 SE's will wander going down a flat long smooth road. It's just enough to be annoying. If you hold the steering straight, it will slowly wander 1 foot left and then slowly recover 1 foot to the right. If you rock the steering, the car will follow but it appears to pivot about the center of the chassis. The car otherwise handles fine. It's not bump steer since there's no bumps. I have other Fiero's to compare to and this is not normal. <P>Everything in the front and rear suspension is new except for the rear struts, rear tie rods and cradle bushings. All ball joints, all bushings, new rack, new dampener, blah, blah. Now before we jump on those, they are good. We've pushed, pryed, pokes, forced and cussed everything.<P>It's been four-wheel aligned three times. The last two times used the *updated* specifications although I am beginning to think they are wrong. <P>It has new stock tires (215/60-14) and rims and we've tried a different set. The chassis is straight (it's been measured several times). All engine mounts are new. Yes, it does have the 3800SC which adds 80 lbs but the battery is moved forward so it's now about 60 lbs. For fun, I put 150lbs in the rear of my other 86SE and it still drives straight.<P>Anyone have any other suggestions? <P>One suggestion I did get was that the alignment shop may not have settled the car enough when they lifted it to make geometry changes. I've been thinking of taking it back down and having them throw it on the machine as see what setting are without lifting it.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000007||danny boy||03-11-2001||03:41 AM|||| Stupid bump steer! Stupid Fiero! Arrgh! Rebush the whole front end and put an '88 cradle under the rear. then throw that worst design ever cradle off the highest cliff you can find! Whew, feel better now.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stealth_feb2.jpg"> ||||reg||8
Z||000008||99BlackZ||03-11-2001||10:58 AM||||This may be too simple, but did you check the air pressure in the rear tires?<BR>I remember mine used to wander a bit from side to side if the tires were low.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula88||03-11-2001||12:41 PM||||Good suggestion 99!  Always start with the simplest answer first.  I'd hate to rebuild my entire suspension and then realize I had a leak in my rear tire!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I'm sure everyone when making recommendations is assuming this has already been checked, but it's good to get a reminder once in a while.||||reg||1
Z||000010||pontiacs4me||03-11-2001||02:31 PM||||check your tire pressure, the same thing happened to my car and I found out that every tire was low at least 15 lbs.  (it sat for quite a while) It had the same handling problem.  ||||reg||1
Z||000011||terryk||03-11-2001||06:31 PM||||As I said, the complete front and rear suspensions have been rebuilt except for the rear tie-rods, cradle bushings and struts and none of them are defective. We've checked them loaded and unloaded. The tire pressure makes no difference up or down.<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 03-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||ToddAsh||03-11-2001||08:53 PM|||| When I got my car it was terrible on the highway.Had it aligned,checked every bushing,changed shocks/struts problem stayed the same. For the heck of it I rotated the tires front to back.These were the original tires with 15,000 miles.Problem gone.I don't know whether the problem was the front or rears but switching them solved the problem.Tires still look great but I don't trust them because of the age.Just switched to 16" wheels and tires. Awsome! A big go-cart. Can't wait to add a rear swaybar. Todd||||reg||1
Z||000013||terryk||03-11-2001||10:34 PM||||Hi Todd,<P>I thought about that too and swapped them front to back and left to right and used two other sets. No change.<P>I'm going to take it in next week and have them measure it without lifting it first. They always want to lift it up before they even measure it the first time. I think that's part of what is wrong. Once they lift it, the suspension doesn't settle back into place without being rolled.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Standard||03-12-2001||12:55 AM||||It's also the basic design of the Fiero and physics.  All of the mass of the car is in the center, which means it can be rotated easily.  Low polar moment of inertia.  Which is why they don't make very good high-speed cruisers.  Also why front engine cars are much more stable at high speeds.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Conrad Sanville||03-12-2001||06:51 AM||||Good tires and tight and properly aligned suspension are a must, especially high-speed driving. It is critical on the Fiero or any mid-engine car for that matter.  <P>Despite the laws of physics, what engine placement is the most popular among the world's greatest racing cars? and what about the rigs that break land speed records on the salt flats?  ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Fierowrecker||03-12-2001||08:33 AM||||Hey Terry!<BR>  Do you drive your car in the winter?<BR>I had the same problem, turns out the rust ate through the frame...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||terryk||03-12-2001||11:55 AM||||Yes, but I'm in California, no salt and it's never seen snow. It's not rusted through. I looked it over well.||||reg||1
